[node name="PanopticonEye" type="Node3D"]
editor_description = "THE EYE. One box, 18 m across and 4.5 m tall, standing on the tower platform with the guard inside it and a picture of an eye repeated along every face. There is no tower and no ball. There was a 31 m socket with lids, a brow and a crown; then a lit red sphere with a black band; then this same box floating 16.7 m overhead. All of it was art nobody asked for. Ryan: 'i dont want the cylinder, all i want is a one way mirror, it can be the simple jpg of an eye four times in a box if needed', and then 'the eye box is right but should be at eye level, and be a one way mirror to cover the shooter.' Git has the rest.

IT IS A ONE-WAY MIRROR, LITERALLY. The box is a closed surface with every triangle facing outward, drawn backface-culled. From the ring every face is front-facing: an opaque wall with an eye on it and no guard behind it. From inside, where the guard is and where nobody else ever is, every face is back-facing and thrown away before it is shaded -- so the guard sees the whole ring through it with no tint, no seam and no pixel lost. That is the same mechanism the old socket drum used; only the scale is new.

WHY IT IS AT y=1.9. Because that is the guard's eye: they spawn at y=0.25 and their head is 1.65 m above their feet. The box is centred there, so its pupils are level with the guard inside it and with every prisoner on the deck outside it. The eye used to float at 16.7 m while the guard sat at 1.9; that disagreement was open all night and Ryan settled it on 2026-09-10 in this direction -- the eye comes down to the guard.

IT COVERS THE SHOOTER. 18 m across over an 8 m platform leaves a metre of margin at the rim in every direction, and the top at y=4.15 clears the guard's crown at the apex of a jump (y=3.16) by about a metre. The underside at y=-0.35 is below the platform surface, so there is no gap to see under.

IT CANNOT BE READ FOR A BEARING. A box has four distinguishable sides, so unlike the old ball this is not true of its shape and is instead true of its surface: one material, one texture, and PanopticonEye.uv_tiling_for spread over the BoxMesh UV atlas so every face draws the same upright copies of the same picture at the same size. Unshaded, so the sun cannot brighten one side over another. Unrotated, and nothing rotates it. See scripts/tower/panopticon_eye.gd and tests/test_tower.gd.

NO COLLISION, ANYWHERE. The guard is not trapped by their own cover and their rifle shoots straight through it."
script = ExtResource("1_eye")
face_material = SubResource("MatFace")
box_path = NodePath("Box")
profile = ExtResource("2_eye_profile")

[node name="Box" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="."]
editor_description = "18 x 4.5 x 18 m, centred on the axis at y=1.9, so it spans y=-0.35 to y=4.15 and x,z=-9 to +9. Square in plan, which matters: an oblong would have a broad side and a narrow one, and a prisoner who could tell which they were standing in front of would know something about the tower.

Four eyes to a face, because a face four times wider than it is tall would otherwise stretch one eye four times wider than it is high, and a stretched eye does not read as an eye. The count is derived from the proportions in PanopticonEye.uv_tiling_for, not typed in.

It is a single BoxMesh rather than four quads on purpose. Four planes would give per-face control nobody wants and would leave the box open at the top and bottom -- and an open box is not opaque, which is the whole job. One closed box is fewer parts and is a one-way mirror from every angle."
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1.9, 0)
mesh = SubResource("MeshBox")
surface_material_override/0 = SubResource("MatFace")
